{
  "dom_id": "step-e3624",
  "name": "Example: Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)=3e^x + \\ln 3$, find $f'(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "The derivative of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right)\\\n                                                  &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( 3e^x\\right) +  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\ln 3 \\right)\\\n                                                  &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0\\\n                                                  &= 3 e^x.\n}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
